  <content>
  <section><title>Equations Without Solutions</title>
<para>What happens when we solve \( 5z = 5z - 1\)?</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Subtract \(5z\) to get the \(z\) terms together on one side.</para>
<para>\(\begin{aligned}5z-5z &amp;=5z-5z-1\end{aligned}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Simplify and the \(z\) terms are gone!.</para>
<para>\(\begin{aligned}0 &amp;=1\end{aligned}\)</para>
<para>Since 0 does not equal \(-1\), this equation led to a false statement. There are no solutions to this equation.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example:</emphasis></para>
<para>Solve \( 5m+3(9+3m)=2(7m−11) \). </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.
  Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses.
  Combine like terms.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis> Check the solution.</para>
<title>Infinitely Many Solutions</title>
<para>What if an equation results in a true statement without variables?</para>
<para>The equations \( -4 = -4 \), \( 5 = 5 \), and \( 0 = 0 \) are all true statements because one side is equal to the other. This means that any value of the variable in the original equation would be a solution. When this happens in the final step of an equation, there are infinitely many solutions.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example:</emphasis> Solve \( 2y +6 =2(y+3) \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.
  Use the Distributive Property to remove any parentheses.
  Combine like terms. </para>
<para>\( 2y +6 =2(y+3) \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation.
  Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
<para>\(2y+6=2y+6\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis> Check the solution.
  We say the solution to the equation is all of the real numbers.An equation that is true for any value of the variable is called an <term data-store="glossary-tooltip" data-schema-version="1.0">identity</term>. </para>
<para>The solution of an identity is all real numbers.</para>
<title>Try It: Equations with No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions </title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
     
    <para>1. Solve \( 10x - 4 = 2(5x -2) \)and determine the number of solutions.</para>
  <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
  <para>Solve this linear equation using a general strategy:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Original equation.</para>
  <para>\( 10x - 4 = 2(5x -2) \)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Distribute</para>
  <para>\( 10x - 4 =10x - 4 \)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality. </para>
  <para>\(\begin{aligned}10x - 10x - 4  &amp;=10x - 10x - 4 \\-4 &amp;=-4 \end{aligned}\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Check the solution</para>
  <para>This is a true statement so there are infinitely many solutions. 
</para></solution></exercise>
</example><example><exercise>
  <problem>
     
    <para>2. Solve \( 3x - 4 = 3(x -2) \) and determine the number of solutions.</para>
  <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
  <para>Solve this linear equation using a general strategy:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Original equation.</para>
  <para>\( 3x - 4 = 3(x -2) \)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2 - </emphasis>Distribute</para>
  <para>\( 3x - 4 = 3x -6 \)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation. Use the Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality. </para>
  <para>\(\begin{aligned}3x - 3x - 4  &amp;=3x - 3x - 6 \\-4 &amp;=-6 \end{aligned}\)</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Check the solution</para>
  <para>The equation \( -4=-6\) is NOT a true statement. The equation \(3x - 4 = 3(x -2) \) has no solutions. </para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
